// FoundryGate usage reconciliation (#39 / #84) — billing-grade per-developer token totals.
//
// Run it by hand: Log Analytics workspace > Logs, set the time range to the billing period, paste.
// The Function passes the period as the query's *time range* (LogsQueryClient's QueryTimeRange, which
// bounds TimeGenerated server-side) rather than as a literal in the text, so this file is
// parameter-free and the same query answers for any period you select in the portal.
//
// Two tables, because neither alone is enough:
//   ApiManagementGatewayLlmLog   per-request prompt/completion/total tokens and the model, from the
//                                "Logs related to generative AI gateway" diagnostic setting.
//                                It does NOT carry the APIM subscription.
//   ApiManagementGatewayLogs     the gateway's request log, which does carry ApimSubscriptionId.
// CorrelationId is the same value on both rows for one request, so it is the join key.
//
// *** One request can produce several rows on BOTH sides — de-duplicate before summing. ***
// The LLM log emits "requests and responses, including chunked messages for large requests and
// responses ... in separate log entries that you can correlate by using the CorrelationId field"
// (learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/api-management-howto-llm-logs), which is what
// SequenceNumber indexes. The token columns are documented per *request*, not per entry —
// "PromptTokens: The number of prompt tokens used by the request"
// (learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-monitor/reference/tables/apimanagementgatewayllmlog) — so the same
// counts repeat across an exchange's entries and a naive sum() multiplies every developer's usage by
// however many entries their requests happened to produce.
//
// The collapse below is max() per column per CorrelationId, deliberately in preference to
// arg_max(SequenceNumber, *):
//   - counts repeated on every entry  -> max is that value (sum would multiply)
//   - counts only on the response entry, 0/null elsewhere -> max is that value
//   - counts only on the last entry -> max is that value
// arg_max(SequenceNumber, *) is right only in the last case, and picks a token-less entry whenever
// the highest sequence number belongs to a chunked request rather than the response. The one shape
// max would under-count is genuinely partitioned per-chunk counts, which the "used by the request"
// wording rules out and #178 checks against a live gateway before this is trusted for billing.
//
// Caveat that survives the de-duplication (documented in #84 and on the architecture overview):
// "In scenarios where token usage is missing (such as a broken stream or terminated connection),
// token usage might not be logged or is inaccurate" — so an interrupted stream can leave a request
// counted low or not at all. These totals are therefore a floor. Enforcement does not depend on them:
// the gateway's own token-quota counter is what returns 403.
ApiManagementGatewayLlmLog
| where isnotempty(CorrelationId)
| summarize
    PromptTokens = max(PromptTokens),
    CompletionTokens = max(CompletionTokens),
    TotalTokens = max(TotalTokens)
  by CorrelationId
| join kind=inner (
    ApiManagementGatewayLogs
    | where isnotempty(ApimSubscriptionId)
    | distinct CorrelationId, ApimSubscriptionId
  ) on CorrelationId
| summarize
    PromptTokens = sum(PromptTokens),
    CompletionTokens = sum(CompletionTokens),
    TotalTokens = sum(TotalTokens),
    RequestCount = count()
  by ApimSubscriptionId
